<h1>Modern CSS layout patterns</h1>

<p>Grid, subgrid, and container queries have changed how we build layouts. This article walks through the patterns used in this starter kit and explains why each choice was made.</p>

<h2>The three-column page grid</h2>

<p>The core layout primitive is a three-column grid on <code>main</code>: a flexible gutter on each side, with a content column capped at <code>65ch</code>. This pattern lets sections control their own bleed behaviour without per-component breakpoints.</p>

<pre><code>main {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns:
    [full-start] minmax(var(--space-md), 1fr)
    [main-start] minmax(0, var(--content-max))
    [main-end]   minmax(var(--space-md), 1fr) [full-end];
}</code></pre>

<p>Named lines (<code>full-start</code>, <code>main-start</code>, etc.) mean sections can opt into full-bleed or stay in the main column by name, not by a magic column count.</p>

<h2>Full-bleed sections with subgrid</h2>

<p>Adding <code>data-bleed</code> to a section extends its background edge-to-edge while snapping content back to the main column using subgrid. No negative margins, no overflow tricks.</p>

